Eczema vs. Psoriasis Symptoms: How Do They Differ?

Symptoms offer useful clues, but their presentation can vary depending on skin tone, age, the affected body area, and disease severity.

1. Itching

Intense itching is a hallmark of eczema, especially atopic dermatitis. It can occur throughout the day and may become particularly troublesome at night. Scratching may provide temporary relief but can damage the skin and worsen inflammation.

Psoriasis can also itch. Some people experience mild irritation, while others have significant itching, burning, or discomfort. However, thickened plaques and clearly defined scales are often more prominent features than itching alone.

If persistent itching is accompanied by very dry skin, inflamed patches, and a history of sensitive skin or allergies, eczema may be one possibility. Raised plaques with thick scaling may suggest psoriasis, but a clinician must assess the full presentation.

2. Redness and Inflammation

Both eczema and psoriasis can cause inflamed skin.

In eczema, the affected area may look red or pink on lighter skin. On darker skin, inflammation can appear violet, grey, dark brown, or darker than the surrounding area. Swelling and tenderness may occur during a flare.

Psoriasis plaques can also vary in colour. They may look red with silvery scales on lighter skin, or violet, brown, grey, or darker than the surrounding skin on darker complexions.

Recognizing inflammation across different skin tones is important because redness may be less obvious in some individuals. Changes in texture, warmth, swelling, and pigmentation can provide additional clues.

3. Scaling and Flaking

Scaling is common in both conditions, but the pattern may differ.

Eczema often causes dry, flaky skin. During a severe flare, affected areas may develop tiny blisters, release fluid, or form crusts. Persistent scratching can eventually cause thickening and more prominent skin lines.

Psoriasis more typically produces raised plaques with visible, layered scales. These scales may be white or silvery on lighter skin and may look grey or lighter than the surrounding skin on darker complexions.

Not every psoriasis patch has thick scales. Inverse psoriasis, for example, may appear smooth and shiny, especially in skin folds.

4. Skin Cracks and Bleeding

Eczema can cause painful cracks, particularly on the hands, feet, and areas exposed to frequent washing or irritating substances. The skin may become so dry that everyday activities cause discomfort.

Psoriasis plaques can also crack, especially when they are thick or located over joints. Cracks may bleed and become painful.

Open or cracked skin is more vulnerable to infection. Increasing pain, spreading redness, warmth, pus, or fever should prompt medical assessment.

5. Skin Thickening and Pigmentation Changes

Repeated inflammation and scratching can make eczema-affected skin thicker and more pronounced in texture. This change is called lichenification. After a flare resolves, lighter or darker patches may remain for weeks or months.

Psoriasis produces thickening through the accumulation of skin cells and inflammation. The affected area may remain raised even when other symptoms improve.

Both conditions can leave pigmentation changes, particularly in darker skin tones. These changes do not necessarily mean that active inflammation is still present, but persistent or worsening symptoms should be assessed.

Where Do Eczema and Psoriasis Usually Appear?

The location of a rash can help a healthcare professional distinguish between eczema and psoriasis.

Common Locations for Eczema

Atopic dermatitis often affects:

The inner elbows and backs of the knees.

The neck and face.

The wrists and ankles.

The hands and fingers.

The skin around the eyes.

Other areas exposed to friction or irritants.

In babies, eczema may affect the cheeks, scalp, and outer surfaces of the arms and legs. The distribution can change as children grow.

Hand eczema is common in people who frequently wash their hands or work with water, detergents, solvents, or other irritating substances. However, not every hand rash is eczema, and persistent cases may require assessment for contact allergy or another skin disorder.

Common Locations for Psoriasis

Psoriasis frequently appears on:

The elbows and knees.

The scalp and hairline.

The lower back.

The palms and soles.

The fingernails and toenails.

Skin folds, including the groin and armpits.

Scalp psoriasis may extend beyond the hairline and produce thick scales. Nail psoriasis can cause small pits, discolouration, thickening, crumbling, or separation of the nail from the nail bed.

Psoriasis in skin folds may be mistaken for eczema, fungal infection, or other inflammatory conditions. A clinical examination can help clarify the cause.

Can You Have Both Eczema and Psoriasis?

Yes. Although eczema and psoriasis are different conditions, a person can experience both.

The two conditions may affect separate areas of the body, or their symptoms may overlap. For example, a person may have typical psoriasis plaques on the elbows and eczema on the hands.

In some cases, the appearance is not clear enough to distinguish one condition from another immediately. This is particularly true when inflammation affects the face, scalp, hands, or skin folds.

Using an unsuitable treatment may worsen symptoms or delay the correct diagnosis. If a rash persists despite treatment, repeatedly returns, or changes in appearance, consult a dermatologist rather than assuming that it is the same condition as before.

What Causes Eczema?

Eczema does not have one single cause. It generally develops through a combination of genetic susceptibility, skin barrier dysfunction, immune system activity, and environmental influences.

1. Skin Barrier Dysfunction

The skin barrier helps prevent moisture loss and limits the entry of irritants and allergens. In atopic dermatitis, this barrier may be less effective.

As moisture escapes, the skin becomes dry and vulnerable. Substances that would not normally cause a reaction may irritate the skin more easily.

Regular use of a suitable moisturizer can help support the barrier and reduce dryness.

2. Genetic Factors

Eczema often runs in families. People with a family history of atopic dermatitis, asthma, or allergic rhinitis may have a greater likelihood of developing atopic eczema.

However, having a family history does not guarantee that someone will develop eczema. Environmental exposures and other biological factors also contribute.

3. Immune System Responses

Immune activity plays an important role in atopic dermatitis. Inflammation can disrupt the skin barrier further, producing itching, redness, and irritation.

This interaction between barrier dysfunction and inflammation helps explain why eczema can persist even when a person avoids obvious irritants.

4. Environmental Irritants and Allergens

Common factors that may trigger or worsen eczema include:

Fragranced soaps and personal care products.

Detergents and household cleaning chemicals.

Certain fabrics, especially when they trap heat or cause friction.

Sweating and overheating.

Cold, dry weather.

Frequent washing or prolonged contact with water.

Dust, pollen, or other allergens in susceptible individuals.

Emotional stress and poor sleep.

Triggers differ between individuals. Not everyone with eczema needs to avoid the same foods, fabrics, or environmental exposures.

Food allergy can coexist with eczema, especially in some children, but food is not the cause of every eczema flare. Eliminating major food groups without professional guidance can lead to nutritional problems and may not improve the skin.

What Causes Psoriasis?

Psoriasis is an immune-mediated condition influenced by genetic and environmental factors. It is not caused by poor hygiene and is not an infectious disease.

1. Immune System Activity

In psoriasis, immune signals promote inflammation and stimulate changes in skin cell production. Skin cells accumulate more quickly than they can shed, producing thickened plaques.

The immune system remains involved even when the condition improves. This is one reason psoriasis may return after a period of clearer skin.

2. Genetic Susceptibility

Psoriasis often runs in families, although people can develop it without any known family history.

Inherited factors can influence how the immune system responds to environmental triggers. Having a relative with psoriasis increases susceptibility but does not determine whether the condition will develop.

3. Infections

Certain infections, particularly streptococcal throat infections, may trigger guttate psoriasis or worsen existing psoriasis in susceptible people.

A sudden outbreak of small, drop-shaped spots following a sore throat should be evaluated by a healthcare professional.

4. Skin Injury

Psoriasis plaques may develop at sites of skin injury, including cuts, scratches, sunburn, or friction. This response is known as the Koebner phenomenon.

Protecting the skin from avoidable injury may help some individuals reduce flares, although it cannot prevent every outbreak.

5. Stress and Lifestyle Factors

Psychological stress may trigger or worsen psoriasis in some people. Smoking, heavy alcohol consumption, and obesity are also associated with psoriasis severity or poorer health outcomes.

These factors do not mean that someone has caused their own condition. Psoriasis is a complex inflammatory disease, and lifestyle changes should complement—not replace—appropriate medical care.

6. Certain Medicines

Some medicines can trigger or aggravate psoriasis in susceptible individuals. Examples may include lithium, certain antimalarial medicines, and some blood pressure medicines.

Do not stop a prescribed medicine without speaking to the clinician who manages your treatment. A healthcare professional can assess whether a medicine may be contributing and discuss appropriate alternatives when necessary.

How Do Doctors Diagnose Eczema and Psoriasis?

There is no single home test that can reliably distinguish eczema from psoriasis. Diagnosis usually involves reviewing symptoms, examining the skin, and considering personal and family medical history.

Medical History

A dermatologist may ask:

When did the symptoms first appear?

Is the itching constant or intermittent?

Which areas of the body are affected?

Does the rash worsen during particular seasons?

Have new soaps, cosmetics, medicines, or cleaning products been introduced?

Is there a family history of eczema or psoriasis?

Have previous treatments helped or made the condition worse?

Are there associated symptoms, such as joint pain or nail changes?

These questions help identify potential triggers and patterns.

Physical Examination

The clinician examines the colour, thickness, borders, scale, and distribution of the affected skin. They may also inspect the scalp, nails, and other areas where symptoms can provide diagnostic clues.

Eczema may be more likely when the skin is dry, intensely itchy, and inflamed, particularly around characteristic areas such as the inner elbows or behind the knees.

Psoriasis may be more likely when there are raised, well-defined plaques with thick scales, particularly on the elbows, knees, or scalp.

However, these patterns are not definitive. Other conditions—including fungal infections, seborrhoeic dermatitis, contact dermatitis, and certain less common skin diseases—can resemble eczema or psoriasis.

Additional Tests

Most cases can be diagnosed through clinical examination. Additional tests may be considered when the diagnosis is uncertain.

Skin biopsy: A small skin sample may be examined under a microscope to help distinguish between conditions.

Patch testing: This may be recommended when allergic contact dermatitis is suspected. Small quantities of potential allergens are applied to the skin under controlled conditions and assessed for reactions.

Skin scraping or other tests: A clinician may test for fungal infection or another condition that resembles an inflammatory rash.

Joint assessment: People with suspected psoriasis who experience joint pain, swelling, or stiffness may need evaluation for psoriatic arthritis.

The aim is to identify the correct condition and select treatment based on the person's symptoms, medical history, and individual needs.

Eczema Treatment Options

The goal of eczema treatment is to restore the skin barrier, reduce inflammation, relieve itching, prevent infections, and reduce the frequency of flares.

Treatment depends on the type of eczema, the affected body areas, age, severity, and response to previous therapies.

1. Moisturizers and Emollients

Regular moisturizing is a central part of eczema management.

Emollients help soften the skin and reduce moisture loss. Options include creams, ointments, and lotions, with thicker formulations often being useful for very dry skin.

Choose products that are fragrance-free and suitable for sensitive skin. Apply moisturizer regularly, especially after bathing or washing, while the skin is still slightly damp.

People with eczema may need to moisturize several times a day, depending on dryness and environmental conditions.

2. Gentle Cleansing and Skin Care

Hot water, harsh soaps, fragrances, and abrasive products can worsen eczema.

Helpful measures include:

Taking short, lukewarm showers or baths.

Using a gentle cleanser when needed.

Avoiding scrubbing the affected skin.

Patting the skin dry rather than rubbing it.

Applying moisturizer immediately after bathing.

Wearing soft clothing that does not cause excessive friction.

Using protective gloves when handling irritating household products, where appropriate.

These steps support the skin barrier but may not be sufficient during a significant flare.

3. Topical Corticosteroids

Topical corticosteroids are commonly used to reduce eczema inflammation during flares.

They are available in different strengths and formulations. The appropriate option depends on the patient's age, the location of the rash, its severity, and how long treatment is needed.

Mild preparations may be suitable for certain areas, while stronger products may be prescribed for more severe inflammation on appropriate body sites.

The face, eyelids, groin, and skin folds require particular care because prolonged or inappropriate use of stronger corticosteroids can cause side effects.

Follow medical instructions about the amount, frequency, and duration of use. Do not use someone else's prescription or continue treatment indefinitely without review.

4. Non-Steroid Topical Medicines

Depending on the type and severity of eczema, clinicians may recommend non-steroid anti-inflammatory medicines.

Topical calcineurin inhibitors, such as tacrolimus or pimecrolimus, may be useful for certain areas, including sensitive skin sites, when appropriate.

Other prescription topical treatments are available for selected patients. Their suitability depends on the specific diagnosis, age, severity, and local prescribing guidance.

A dermatologist can explain the benefits, possible side effects, and correct application of each option.

5. Treatment for Moderate or Severe Eczema

When eczema is extensive, persistent, or not adequately controlled by topical treatment, a clinician may consider phototherapy or systemic medicines.

Options may include biologic medicines, oral targeted treatments, or other immunomodulating medicines, depending on the patient's circumstances and local availability.

These treatments require medical assessment and monitoring because they have different risks, contraindications, and follow-up requirements.

Oral corticosteroids are generally not preferred as a routine long-term treatment for atopic dermatitis because symptoms can rebound after they are stopped and prolonged use can cause significant side effects.

6. Managing Itching

Itching can make eczema particularly difficult to manage. Keeping the skin moisturized, applying prescribed anti-inflammatory treatment, and using cool compresses may help.

Keeping fingernails short can reduce skin damage from scratching. Cotton gloves may be useful for some children at night, provided they are safe and comfortable.

Antihistamines do not directly treat the underlying skin inflammation. A clinician may recommend them in selected circumstances, but they are not a universal solution for eczema-related itching.

7. Preventing Skin Infections

Scratching can break the skin and increase the risk of infection. Warning signs include increasing pain, spreading redness, warmth, pus, yellowish crusting, or fever.

Some eczema infections require prescription treatment. Repeatedly applying antibiotic creams without medical advice is not recommended because these products can cause contact allergy and contribute to antibiotic resistance.

Rapidly worsening eczema accompanied by painful blisters, widespread erosions, or fever needs urgent medical assessment.

Psoriasis Treatment Options

Psoriasis treatment aims to reduce inflammation, control scaling, improve comfort, and maintain periods of better skin control. The best treatment depends on the type of psoriasis, its severity, the body areas involved, and whether joints or nails are affected.

1. Moisturizers and Scale Management

Moisturizers can help reduce dryness, soften plaques, and improve skin comfort. Although they do not address every aspect of psoriasis, they can complement prescription treatment.

Products containing ingredients such as urea or salicylic acid may help soften thick scales in selected cases. However, these products can irritate sensitive skin, and salicylic acid may not be suitable for every patient or body area.

Ask a clinician or pharmacist which products are appropriate, particularly when treating children, large skin areas, or sensitive locations.

2. Topical Corticosteroids

Topical corticosteroids are frequently prescribed for localized plaque psoriasis.

They reduce inflammation and may help flatten plaques and decrease itching. Different strengths and formulations are used depending on the affected area.

Stronger preparations may be suitable for certain thick plaques for limited periods, whereas the face, skin folds, and other sensitive sites generally require more cautious treatment.

Do not assume that a product prescribed for one area is appropriate for another. Follow the recommended schedule and seek advice if symptoms return frequently.

3. Vitamin D Analogues

Topical vitamin D analogues, such as calcipotriol, can help regulate skin cell growth and reduce psoriasis plaques.

They are sometimes used alone or in combination with topical corticosteroids. The treatment plan depends on the location and severity of the condition.

These medicines are different from ordinary vitamin supplements. Taking vitamin D tablets is not an equivalent treatment for plaque psoriasis unless a clinician has identified another reason to prescribe them.

4. Other Topical Treatments

Depending on the condition, a clinician may prescribe other topical medicines, including calcineurin inhibitors for selected sensitive areas or additional products designed to control scaling and inflammation.

The choice depends on the psoriasis subtype and the patient's individual needs. Treatment for psoriasis on the scalp, palms, soles, nails, or skin folds may require a different approach from treatment for plaques on the elbows or knees.

5. Phototherapy

Phototherapy uses carefully controlled ultraviolet light to treat certain forms of psoriasis.

Narrowband UVB is one option for people with more widespread disease who have not responded adequately to topical treatment or for whom topical treatment is impractical.

Phototherapy should be provided under appropriate medical supervision. Uncontrolled sun exposure and tanning beds are not safe substitutes because ultraviolet exposure can damage the skin and increase skin cancer risk.

6. Systemic Medicines and Biologic Treatments

Moderate to severe psoriasis may require systemic treatment, particularly when large areas are affected or the disease significantly interferes with daily life.

Options may include conventional systemic medicines, targeted oral therapies, and biologic medicines that act on specific immune pathways.

Some treatments require blood tests or other monitoring. A clinician will consider factors such as other medical conditions, pregnancy plans, infection risks, previous treatment response, and potential drug interactions.

Biologic medicines can be highly effective for many patients, but they are not suitable for everyone and require ongoing professional supervision.

7. Treatment for Psoriatic Arthritis

People with psoriasis who experience persistent joint pain, swelling, morning stiffness, or changes in finger or toe shape should seek medical assessment.

Psoriatic arthritis can develop before, alongside, or after skin symptoms. Early recognition is important because untreated inflammatory joint disease can cause lasting damage.

A dermatologist, rheumatologist, or primary care clinician can help coordinate appropriate evaluation and treatment.

Eczema vs. Psoriasis: Which Treatment Is Better?

Neither condition has one universally best treatment. The correct approach depends on the diagnosis and the person's individual circumstances.

Eczema treatment primarily focuses on improving the skin barrier, controlling inflammation, relieving itching, and reducing exposure to triggers. Moisturizers and appropriate topical anti-inflammatory treatments are central to care.

Psoriasis treatment focuses on reducing inflammation and controlling abnormal skin cell turnover. Depending on severity, options may include topical corticosteroids, vitamin D analogues, phototherapy, and systemic or biologic medicines.

Some treatments overlap, but this does not mean they are interchangeable. A treatment that helps one condition may be ineffective or unsuitable for another.

For example, using a moisturizer may benefit both conditions, but it is unlikely to control significant psoriasis or severe eczema by itself. Likewise, prescription corticosteroids can help both conditions in selected situations, but the formulation, strength, and treatment duration may differ.

The most effective first step is obtaining an accurate diagnosis rather than experimenting with multiple products.

Home Care Tips for Eczema and Psoriasis

Home care can support prescribed treatment and improve daily comfort. However, home remedies should not replace medical assessment when symptoms are persistent, severe, or uncertain.

Moisturize Consistently

Choose a fragrance-free moisturizer suited to your skin. Apply it regularly, including after washing or bathing.

If a product causes burning, swelling, or worsening irritation, stop using it and seek advice if the reaction persists.

Identify Personal Triggers

Keeping a symptom diary may help reveal patterns involving weather, sweating, stress, washing products, or workplace exposures.

Avoid making major dietary restrictions based only on suspected food triggers. If a food allergy is a concern, discuss appropriate evaluation with a qualified healthcare professional.

Reduce Scratching

Use cool compresses for temporary relief and keep nails short. Treating the underlying inflammation is usually more effective than relying on scratching alternatives alone.

Protect Your Skin

Use gentle cleansers and avoid harsh exfoliation. Wear comfortable clothing, protect the skin from avoidable injury, and use suitable protective equipment when working with irritating substances.

Manage Stress and Sleep

Stress does not cause every flare, but it may worsen symptoms for some people. Regular sleep, physical activity appropriate to your health, relaxation techniques, and support from friends or family may improve overall well-being.

Be Careful With Unverified Remedies

Natural oils, herbal creams, essential oils, and online skin treatments may be marketed as cures. Evidence varies, and some products can cause irritation or allergic contact dermatitis.

Avoid applying bleach, strong acids, undiluted essential oils, or other harsh substances to affected skin unless a specific treatment has been medically prescribed for a clearly defined purpose.

When Should You See a Dermatologist?

Consider arranging a medical appointment if:

A rash persists or repeatedly returns.

You cannot determine whether the problem is eczema, psoriasis, or another skin condition.

Itching interferes with sleep or everyday activities.

The skin becomes painful, deeply cracked, or extensively inflamed.

Over-the-counter skin care products do not provide adequate relief.

A rash spreads rapidly or affects the face, eyelids, or genital area.

Symptoms worsen after using a new medicine or skin product.

You develop nail changes or persistent joint pain alongside suspected psoriasis.

A child develops a persistent or severe rash.

Seek urgent medical care for widespread redness with fever or feeling seriously unwell, rapidly worsening painful skin, or extensive blistering and skin loss. These symptoms may indicate a serious complication.

People with psoriasis should also seek prompt assessment for a sudden widespread eruption of pus-filled bumps or rapidly developing generalized redness and scaling. Some forms of psoriasis can become medically serious.

Can Eczema and Psoriasis Be Cured?

Eczema and psoriasis are both conditions that can follow a long-term, relapsing course, although their individual patterns differ.

Some children with atopic dermatitis improve substantially as they grow older, while others continue to experience symptoms into adulthood. Eczema may also occur for the first time in adulthood.

Psoriasis can improve for extended periods with treatment, but symptoms may return. The frequency and severity of flares vary considerably.

Neither condition has a guaranteed permanent cure. However, many people achieve substantial symptom improvement through appropriate treatment, skin care, trigger management, and ongoing medical support.

Treatment goals should focus on controlling inflammation, reducing discomfort, preventing complications, and improving quality of life.

Frequently Asked Questions About Eczema vs. Psoriasis

1. What is the main difference between eczema and psoriasis?

Eczema is commonly associated with a weakened skin barrier, dryness, inflammation, and intense itching. Psoriasis is an immune-mediated condition that causes faster skin cell turnover, often resulting in raised, well-defined plaques with thick scales. Both conditions can itch and produce inflamed skin, so a medical assessment may be needed to distinguish them.

2. Is eczema or psoriasis more itchy?

Eczema is often associated with particularly intense itching, which may interfere with sleep. Psoriasis can also cause significant itching, burning, or discomfort. The level of itching varies according to the individual and the severity of the condition.

3. Can eczema turn into psoriasis?

Eczema does not ordinarily transform into psoriasis because they are different conditions. However, their symptoms can overlap, and someone may develop both. If a rash changes appearance or fails to respond to treatment, a dermatologist can reassess the diagnosis.

4. Are eczema and psoriasis contagious?

No. Neither eczema nor psoriasis is contagious. They cannot be spread through touching, sharing clothing, or ordinary social contact. Some infections can trigger or worsen certain inflammatory skin conditions, but that does not make eczema or psoriasis infectious.

5. Can stress cause eczema or psoriasis?

Stress may trigger or worsen flares in some people with eczema or psoriasis. However, stress is not the sole cause of either condition. Genetic susceptibility, immune activity, skin barrier function, environmental exposures, and other factors contribute to their development.

6. Can eczema and psoriasis affect the scalp?

Yes. Eczema-related conditions, particularly seborrhoeic dermatitis and atopic dermatitis, can affect the scalp. Scalp psoriasis may produce thick, clearly defined plaques with visible scaling. The two can sometimes look similar, and other conditions, including fungal infections, may also cause scalp symptoms.

7. Which condition is more serious?

Neither condition is automatically more serious in every case. Mild eczema and mild psoriasis may be manageable with topical treatment, while severe forms of either can substantially affect quality of life.

Psoriasis can be associated with psoriatic arthritis and other health conditions. Severe eczema can lead to skin infections, sleep disruption, and significant discomfort. The severity and complications of each person's condition determine the level of care needed.

8. Can diet improve eczema or psoriasis?

A balanced diet supports general health, but no single diet reliably cures eczema or psoriasis. Some people have specific food allergies or notice individual dietary associations, but unnecessary restrictions can create nutritional problems.

Discuss suspected food triggers with a qualified healthcare professional, particularly when considering dietary changes for children.

9. Can I use the same cream for eczema and psoriasis?

Not necessarily. Some treatments, such as moisturizers and certain prescription anti-inflammatory medicines, may be used for both conditions. However, the appropriate product, strength, location, and duration may differ.

A cream that helps one condition may not adequately treat another. Confirm the diagnosis and follow the instructions provided by your clinician or pharmacist.

10. When should I seek urgent medical attention?

Seek urgent assessment if a rash is rapidly worsening, the skin becomes extensively painful or blistered, or symptoms occur with fever or significant illness. Widespread redness and scaling, particularly in someone with psoriasis, may require urgent treatment.

Persistent joint swelling or stiffness in someone with psoriasis also warrants prompt medical evaluation.

Conclusion

Understanding eczema vs. psoriasis is important for recognizing symptoms, choosing appropriate skin care, and seeking the right treatment. Although both conditions can cause dryness, itching, inflammation, and scaling, they have different underlying mechanisms and may respond differently to treatment.

Eczema commonly involves a weakened skin barrier and an itch-scratch cycle. Its management often emphasizes regular moisturizing, gentle cleansing, trigger management, and appropriate anti-inflammatory treatment.

Psoriasis involves immune-mediated inflammation and accelerated skin cell turnover. Treatment may include moisturizers, topical medicines, phototherapy, or systemic therapies, depending on the severity and the areas affected.

Neither condition should be diagnosed solely from an online description or photograph. Other skin disorders can resemble eczema or psoriasis, and some individuals may have overlapping conditions.

If you experience a persistent rash, severe itching, thickened plaques, nail changes, or joint symptoms, consult a qualified healthcare professional or dermatologist. An accurate diagnosis and an individualized treatment plan can help control symptoms, reduce complications, and improve everyday comfort.

With appropriate medical guidance, consistent skin care, and attention to personal triggers, many people with eczema or psoriasis can manage their symptoms and maintain a good quality of life.

